'''The Homewood''' is a [[Modern architecture|modernist]] house near [[Esher]], Surrey, England. Designed by [[Patrick Gwynne]] for his parents, it was completed in 1938. The house became a Grade II-[[listed building]] in 1971 and was given by Gwynne to the [[National Trust]] in 1999.

==History==
Alban Gwynne, a commander in the [[Royal Navy]], and his wife, Ruby Gywnne ({{née|Bond}}), moved to Homewood<!--The Victorian house bought by the Gwynnes in 1914 was called "Homewood"; the modernist house designed by Patrick Gwynne is called "The Homewood"--> in [[Esher]], Surrey, in 1914. At first they rented the property, but later purchased it outright.{{sfn|Bingham|2004|p=4}} By the mid-1930s, the original Victorian house was in need of repairs and, with noise levels from the adjacent [[A3 road|A3]] road increasing, the Gwynnes chose to build a replacement dwelling on a new site on the northern part of their land.{{sfn|Architectural Review|1939|pp=103–104}}

The new house, called The Homewood, was designed by [[Patrick Gwynne]], a 24-year-old architect and the son of Alban and Ruby Gwynne.<ref name=Bingham_1993>{{cite magazine |last= Bingham |first= Neil |date= July 1993 |title= The Homewood, Surrey |magazine= Country Life |volume= CLXXXVII |issue= 29 |pages= 84–87 }}</ref> The family sold land that Alban Gwynne had inherited in [[Aberaeron]], Wales, to finance the construction work.<ref name=ODNB>{{cite ODNB |last= Howard |first= Sarah C. |date= 22 September 2011 |title= Gwynne, (Alban) Patrick (1913–2003) |doi= 10.1093/ref:odnb/89979}}</ref> Kenneth Powell, a journalist, writes that Gwynne intended the new house "to suit a comfortable upper-middle class life, with plenty of space for entertaining, and also a servants' wing."<ref name=Powell_2001>{{cite news |last= Powell |first= Kenneth |date= 3 June 2001 |title= A House Built to Live In |work= Sunday Telegraph |issue= 2086 |at= Review p. 9 }}</ref>

Patrick Gwynne began to design The Homewood in 1937, shortly after he had left the practice of [[Wells Coates]], a [[modern architecture|modernist]] architect.<ref name=Bingham_1993/> While employed by Coates, he had worked with [[Denys Lasdun]] and [[Edric Neel]], and had been involved in the design of [[Embassy Court]], Hove, and Shipwrights, a private house in [[South Benfleet|Benfleet]], Essex.{{sfn|Bingham|2004|p=8}}

The construction of the new house designed by Gwynne began in October 1937 and was completed the following year.<ref name=ODNB/>{{sfn|Bingham|2023|pp=25–26}} Coates made suggestions on the design, and also recommended contractors with experience of building modernist houses.<ref name=Bingham_1993/> The consulting engineers were Felix Samuely and Conrad Hamann.{{sfn|Architectural Review|1939|p=114}} After the house was completed, Coates insisted that he should be given equal, joint credit for the building, although Gwynne maintained that the design was his alone.{{sfn|Bingham|2023|pp=25–26}}{{sfn|Bingham|2023|pp=127–128}}

The Gwynne family hosted a [[housewarming party]] at The Homewood in July 1938, but were only able to live there for about a year. When the Second World War broke out, Alban Gwynne was recalled to the Royal Navy, and Patrick and his sister enlisted in the armed forces. Ruby Gwynne moved to a cottage in the grounds of the property, allowing the house to be let.{{sfn|Bingham|2004|p=11}} Among the private tenants who rented The Homewood were Robert Lutyens, son of [[Edwin Lutyens]], and the Ambassador of Chile.{{sfn|Bingham|2023|pp=39–40}} Alban and Ruby Gwynne died in 1942, within ten days of each other.{{sfn|Bingham|2004|p=11}}{{sfn|Bingham|2023|pp=39–40}}

Patrick Gwynne took up residence in The Homewood again in 1945. Initially he lived with his sister, Babs, who on her marriage the following year, moved with her husband to the cottage in the grounds, and later to [[Essex]].{{sfn|Bingham|2004|p=11}} Gywnne lived in the house for the next 58 years, also using it as a base for his architectural practice, having converted the master bedroom into an office for his assistants.{{sfn|Bingham|2004|p=11}}<ref name=Owens_2004>{{cite news |last= Owens |first= Mitchell |date= 4 April 2004 |title= A Modernist House Survives in Britain |work= Chicago Tribune |issue= 95 |volume= 157 |at= Section 15 p. 13 }}</ref> He repartitioned other rooms in the same wing, to create two larger bedrooms from three smaller ones.{{sfn|Bingham|2023|p=117}} In the 1960s, the original walnut panelling in the sitting room was replaced with Indian laurel,<ref name=Owens_2004/> and in around 1970, the kitchen was enlarged and refitted.<ref name=Powell_1994>{{cite news |last= Powell |first= Kenneth |date= 30 January 1994 |title= Modernist Bequest to the Nation |work= Sunday Telegraph |issue= 1704 |at= Review p. 28 }}</ref> The house became a Grade II-[[listed building]] on 28 January 1971.<ref>{{NHLE |num= 1365884 |desc= The Homewood |grade= II |fewer-links= yes }}</ref>

The [[National Trust]] agreed, in principle, to preserve The Homewood and its contents in January 1994. The decision was controversial, as some members of the board observed that the trust already had a large number of houses in its ownership, and others considered that modernist buildings were outside the remit of the organisation.<ref name=Powell_2001/><ref name=Powell_1994/> Negotiations with Gwynne lasted until 1999, when The Homewood was formally acquired. The trust began an extensive renovation of the property, overseen by John Allen with Gwynne acting as a consultant.<ref name=Powell_2001/> The work included the restoration of the master bedroom to its original appearance<ref name=Owens_2004/> and the complete replacement of the flat roof.<ref name=Powell_2001/>

Patrick Gwynne died in May 2003 and his [[ash#Cremation ashes|ashes]] were buried in the garden of the Homewood the following March.{{sfn|Bingham|2004|pp=30–31}} A condition for the National Trust acquiring the property was that it should be let to a family, and that it should be opened to the public for one day a week, six months of the year. The house was opened for the first time in March 2004.<ref name=Owens_2004/><ref>{{Cite news |last= Campbell |first= Sophie |date= 7 June 2008 |title= Modernist man required for dream house |work= The Daily Telegraph |at= Travel p. T19  }}</ref>

==Description==
===Design overview===
[[File: VillaSavoye.jpg|thumb|right|Villa Savoye, designed by [[Le Corbusier]] and completed in 1931]]
The Homewood was designed in the [[modern architecture|modernist]] style by [[Patrick Gwynne]] in the late 1930s. Gwynne was influenced by the work of [[Le Corbusier]], a Swiss-French architect and pioneer of modern architecture in the early 20th century, and his design of the [[Villa Savoye]], on the outskirts of Paris, in particular.{{sfn|Bingham|2004|p=8}} Neil Bingham, Gwynne's biographer and an architecture historian, recognises the influence of the Villa Savoye in the east side of the bedroom wing of The Homewood,<ref name=Bingham_1993/> and Kenneth Powell, an architectural critic and historian, writes that Gwynne's design "is a free interpretation of that iconic Corbusier house, an all-white block apparently floating on slender columns."<ref name=Powell_2001/> Other influences on The Homewood include [[Ludwig Mies van der Rohe]] and [[Serge Chermayeff]].<ref name=Powell_2001/>{{sfn|Bingham|2023|p=30}}

[[File:Homewood 02.JPG|thumb|right|The south-facing principal elevation of The Homewood: The living room is the room on the first floor nearest the camera.]]
The Homewood was built on an area of higher ground, close to the northern boundary of the Gwynnes's property, allowing the windows of the living room to face south, with views across the garden.{{sfn|Bingham|2004|p=8}} The house is built as a two-storey bungalow, with all principal rooms, except the owner's [[study (room)|study]], on the upper floor.{{sfn|Architectural Review|1939|pp=103–104}} It has two wings, which are connected by the entrance hall and spiral staircase: The longer wing contains the family and guest bedrooms, and the study; the L-shaped wing contains the living and dining room, service facilities such as the kitchen, and staff accommodation.{{sfn|Bingham|2004|p=8}}

Lydia Greeves, a writer and architectural historian, notes that although The Homewood is "uncompromisingly modern, the house is based on a classical system of proportions and has a pleasing sense of order and harmony."{{sfn|Greeves|2021|p=180}} The internal, [[reinforced concrete]] frame is based on a three-dimensional grid pattern with spacings of {{cvt|1|ft|8|in|m}} in the vertical direction and {{cvt|4|ft|m}} in the horizontal directions, and allows large expanses of open floor and wall space.<ref name=Bingham_1993/>{{sfn|Bingham|2004|p=8}} The upper floor is supported primarily by [[piloti]] (columns), although the walls enclosing the ground floor rooms are of brick.{{sfn|Bingham|2004|p=8}}{{sfn|Architectural Review|1939|p=106}} The main concrete superstructure is painted ivory white.{{sfn|Architectural Review|1939|p=106}} Since the [[water table]] is only {{cvt|5|ft|m}} below ground level, the [[foundation (engineering)|foundations]] were constructed using vertical [[piling|piles]] tied together with horizontal beams.{{sfn|Architectural Review|1939|p=114}} The flat roof was originally covered in [[teak]] slats and had curved windbreaks to provide privacy for rooftop sunbathing.<ref name=Bingham_1993/>{{sfn|Architectural Review|1939|p=115}}

===Entrance block and living room wing===
[[File:Homewood 09.JPG|thumb|right|The main entrance hall with the spiral staircase and the uplighter recessed into the floor]]
The main entrance of The Homewood is accessed from the covered, circular driveway. The front door is set in a [[glass block|glass-block]] wall, which according to Bingham: "dramatically encases the door in light."{{sfn|Bingham|2004|p=16}}{{sfn|Architectural Review|1939|p=107}} The spiral staircase, constructed in concrete with [[terrazzo]] surfaces, is lit from below by an uplighter, embedded in the floor, and from above by an antique glass [[chandelier]] containing [[Bristol blue glass]].{{sfn|Bingham|2004|p=16}}{{sfn|Architectural Review|1939|p=107}}{{efn|The glass [[chandelier]] on the upper landing of The Homewood previously hung in the Gwynnes's original house.<ref name=Bingham_1993/>}} The upper landing links the two wings of the house and the front- and rear-facing walls are glazed.{{sfn|Bingham|2004|p=16}}

Padded, white leather doors lead from the upstairs landing of the entrance block into the living room.<ref name=Bingham_1993/> Gwynne designed the living room as a flexible open-plan space for entertaining and relaxing, and the furniture can be rearranged to suit different uses in different seasons.{{sfn|Greeves|2021|p=180}}{{sfn|Architectural Review|1939|p=110}} The south wall is composed of three multi-panel window units, separated by the concrete framework of the house, which provide a view over the terrace and garden.{{sfn|Architectural Review|1939|p=110}}{{efn|Neil Bingham suggests that the large windows forming the south wall of the living room were inspired by a similar windows at [[10 Palace Gate]], London, an apartment block designed by [[Wells Coates]].{{sfn|Bingham|2023|p=33}}}} The fireplace, which provides the principal focus in the winter months, was inspired by Mies van der Rohe, and is set in a wall of green marble from [[Levanto, Liguria|Levanto]], Italy.<ref name=Bingham_1993/>{{sfn|Bingham|2023|p=30}}{{sfn|Architectural Review|1939|p=110}} The Indian laurel wood panelling on the north wall includes insert shelving and cupboards to store and display items including books, [[phonograph record|records]] and a [[radiogram (device)|radiogram]].{{sfn|Bingham|2023|p=30}}{{sfn|Greeves|2021|p=180}} The maple wood floor is [[sprung floor|sprung for dancing]], using a rubber [[underlay]].<ref name=Powell_2001/><ref name=Owens_2004/>

Much of the furniture in the living room was designed by Gwynne in the 1960s, including the Chinese-style [[lacquer]]ed book table,<ref name=Bingham_1993/>{{sfn|Bingham|2004|p=19}} desk, side table and a long table incorporating a [[cine film]] editor.{{sfn|Bingham|2004|p=20}} Also by Gwynne, but dating from the late 1930s are the glass-topped occasional table and semi-circular sofa.{{sfn|Bingham|2004|p=20}} Items designed by others include two [[chaise longue|chaises longues]] by [[Bruno Mathsson]], a [[Bauhaus]] chair by [[Marcel Breuer]],{{sfn|Bingham|2004|p=20}} and a leather [[Eames Lounge Chair]] with matching [[Ottoman (furniture)|Ottoman]], which had previously belonged to [[Laurence Harvey]].{{sfn|Bingham|2004|p=19}} Artworks in the living room include a drawing by [[Stefan Knapp]]{{sfn|Bingham|2004|p=19}} and a bronze sculpture entitled ''The Japanese Actor'' by Oliffe Richmond.{{sfn|Bingham|2004|p=20}}

[[File:Homewood 08.JPG|thumb|right|upright|The dining room balcony (above) and swimming pool from the west]]
The living room is separated from the dining room partly by a [[folding door|folding screen door]], with decoration by Peter Thompson, and partly by the fixed wall containing the fireplace. The side of the screen facing the living room is painted with images of bamboo and the other side has images of [[corncob|maize cobs]], picked out in [[gold leaf]].{{sfn|O'Brien|Nairn|Cherry|2022|p=302}}{{sfn|Bingham|2004|p=22}} In the centre of the dining room is the round dining table, designed by Gwynne in the 1960s.<ref name=Bingham_1993/>{{sfn|Bingham|2004|p=23}}{{efn|The round dining table has an aluminium base and glass top. It has a central circular well that incorporates coloured lights and is designed to display decorative objects such as flowers.<ref name=Bingham_1993/>{{sfn|Bingham|2004|p=23}}}} The dining chairs, covered in white vinyl, are by [[Eero Saarinen]], and the four portraits of members of the Gwynne family were painted by [[Mather Brown]] in around 1810.{{sfn|Bingham|2004|p=23}} A [[serving hatch]] allows food to be passed directly from the kitchen,<ref name=Bingham_1993/> and a door provides access to the servants' quarters.{{sfn|Bingham|2004|p=22}} Glass double doors on the west side of the dining room lead to the balcony that overlooks the swimming pool.{{sfn|Bingham|2004|p=23}}

===Bedroom wing===
[[File:Homewood 11.JPG|thumb|right|The bedroom wing from the north east]]
With the exception of Patrick Gwynne's office and its associated cloakroom and dedicated entrance hall, all of the rooms in the bedroom wing are on the first floor.{{sfn|Bingham|2023|p=117}}{{sfn|Bingham|2004|p=26}} Neil Bingham suggests that the design of the wing is a "homage to Shipwrights", the construction of which was supervised by Gwynne in 1937.{{sfn|Bingham|2023|p=28}} The curve of the east elevation of the wing is interrupted by a pair of balconies, which Gwynne described as "pulled-out pieces of concrete-like drawers".{{sfn|Bingham|2023|p=30}} The windows on the western side side of the wing, overlooking the terrace, are narrower than those on the eastern side.{{sfn|Bingham|2023|p=28}}

When The Homewood was built, the wing contained six bedrooms (including the master bedroom suite), but three of the smaller rooms were repartitioned in the 1950s to give a total of five bedrooms.{{sfn|Bingham|2023|p=117}}{{sfn|Bingham|2004|p=26}}{{efn|Neil Bingham suggests that Patrick Gwynne had originally intended the bedroom wing to have five bedrooms, including the master bedroom suite, but that it was built with six to allow his maternal grandmother to live with the family.{{sfn|Bingham|2004|p=26}}}} The two northernmost bedrooms, one of which has an ensuite bathroom, contain much of their original furniture including cream leather single beds and mahogany bedside tables.{{sfn|Bingham|2004|p=26}} Also on the first floor of the wing is the Powder Room, which provided a space for guests not staying overnight to change for dinner or a party.{{sfn|Bingham|2004|p=26}}

[[File:Homewood 01.JPG|thumb|right|Gwynne's office on the ground floor of the bedroom wing]]
The office was originally designed for Gwynne's father. Its position, on the ground floor of the bedroom wing, and dedicated entrance, meant that it could be accessed by clients and business associates without having to enter the main part of the house.{{sfn|Bingham|2004|p=28}} The current appearance of the office is from a refitting during the 1960s. It contains a circular table and desk, both designed by Gwynne, and chairs by Eero Saarinen, [[Arne Jacobsen]] and [[Harry Bertoia]].{{sfn|Bingham|2023|p=111}} Three drawings by [[Henri Gaudier-Brzeska]] hang on the walls.{{sfn|Bingham|2004|p=28}}

The driveway for vehicle access to the house runs beneath the northern part of the bedroom wing.{{sfn|Architectural Review|1939|p=104}}

===Terrace and garden===
The terrace runs the length of the south front of the living room wing and incorporates the [[loggia]] beneath the rooms above. It links the house to the garden, providing an outdoor living space. It can be reached both via a staircase from the dining room balcony{{sfn|Architectural Review|1939|p=116}}{{sfn|Bingham|2004|p=29}} and from the driveway via a lobby area beneath the servants' quarters.{{sfn|Architectural Review|1939|p=104}}{{sfn|Bingham|2004|p=28}}{{efn|The ground-floor lobby leading to the terrace features a mosaic of glass tiles, in which the words "The Homewood Esher Surrey Patrick Gwynne" appear in black, highly stylised lettering, superimposed on orange tiles that depict the plan of the house.{{sfn|Bingham|2004|p=28}}{{sfn|Bingham|2004|p=29}}{{sfn|Treasures from the National Trust|2004|p=206}}}} The oval, mosaic-tiled pool was designed by Denys Lasdun as a housewarming gift for the Gwynne family.<ref name=Bingham_1993/>{{sfn|O'Brien|Nairn|Cherry|2022|p=302}} The separate swimming pool, designed by Gwynne, was installed in 1974. At the same time, a former servant's bedroom on the ground floor was converted to a [[sauna]], and the paved loggia became a dining area with an outdoor kitchen.{{sfn|Bingham|2023|p=109}}
